867:
The following uses of conditions shall be and are hereby prohibited within wellhead protection zones, whether or not such use or condition may otherwise be ordinarily included as a part of a use permitted under section 56-196:
(1) Surface use or storage of hazardous material, expressly including commercial use of agricultural pesticides;
(2) Septic tanks or drain fields appurtenant thereto;
(3) Impervious surfaces other than roofs of buildings and street and driveways and walks serving buildings permitted under section 56-196
(4) Sanitary landfills;
(5) Hazardous waste disposal sites;
(6) Stormwater infiltration basins;
(7) Underground storage tanks; or
(8) Sanitary sewer lines within 150 feet of a wellhead.
(Ord. No. 508, § 5, 8-5-2003)

885:
(a) The city reserves the right to terminate water and wastewater disposal services and disconnect a customer from the system when:
(1) Acids or chemicals damaging to sewer lines or treatment process are released into the public sewer causing rapid deterioration of these structures or interfering with proper conveyance and treatment of wastewater;
(2) A governmental agency informs the city that the effluent from the wastewater treatment plant is no longer of a quality permitted for discharge into a watercourse, and it is found that the customer is discharging wastewater into the public sewer that cannot be sufficiently treated or requires treatment that is not provided by the city as normal domestic treatment; or
(3) The customer:
a. Discharges industrial waste or wastewater that is in violation of the permit issued by the approving authority;
b. Discharges wastewater at an uncontrolled, variable rate in sufficient quantity to cause an imbalance in the wastewater treatment process;
c. Fails to pay monthly bills for sanitary sewer service when due; or
d. Repeats a discharge of prohibited wastes into public sewers.
(b) Notification processes for discontinuance of service are presented in section 56-250(m).
(Code 1986, § 25-82; Ord. No. 344, art. 13, 12-7-1987)

976:
At any time it becomes necessary for the police department to remove any vehicle for any cause, the police radio operator, or such person as may be designated by the chief, shall engage the services of those on the rotational call list by calling them in order of call to wit: Service A, Service B, Service C, Service A, Service B, Service C and in rotation provided. However, a deviation from this order may occur and be permitted if:
(1) The next rotation wrecker service does not have a wrecker available with sufficient capacity to handle the call;
(2) The owner or person in possession of a vehicle or person in possession of a vehicle about to be removed designates a wrecker service of his or her preference;
(3) In the event the wrecker service called in rotation does not respond within 20 minutes after receiving the call, the next designated wrecker service in the rotation shall be called;
In the event such person designates a wrecker service of his or her preference, that wrecker service shall be called whether or not it is next on the rotational call list, and such designation shall not interfere with such wrecker's position on the call list; however, if it is necessary to skip over one or more wrecker services because they do not have a wrecker with sufficient capacity to handle a particular call, the wrecker service or services skipped over shall not lose position on the rotation list, but the wrecker service which handles the call shall lose position.
(Code 1986, § 26-85; Ord. No. 422, § 15, 5-4-1993)
